    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To get your roof claim approved, first document the damage and temporarily patch any leaks to prevent further interior destruction. Immediately contact your insurer to file the claim, and hire a local, reputable Utah roofing contractor to be present during your insurance adjuster's inspection so they can verify the full extent of the damage.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "When navigating a roof insurance claim in Utah, consider these critical steps and local insights:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Compare Repair Costs to Deductibles: If the total repair cost is lower than your deductible, filing a claim may not make financial sense. However, you are still required to report unrepaired damage to your carrier.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Look for Local Help: Utah sees its fair share of \"storm chasers\"\u2014out-of-town roofing crews that offer quick fixes and often leave before the job is done correctly. Work with established, local Utah roofing companies that offer insurance claim assistance and free inspections.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Understand Time Limits: While some carriers have short 30-day windows to file claims, most Utah insurance policies allow up to one year from the \"Date of Loss\" (when the storm or damage occurred) to file.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "State Regulations: In Utah, insurance companies generally have 30 days to accept or deny a claim once they receive all required documentation.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Appealing Denials: Common claim denials are due to insufficient documentation, lack of maintenance, or pre-existing conditions. If you experience a dispute with your insurer over the payout, you can request assistance directly from the Utah Insurance Department\n\n.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "Utah Insurance Department",
                "link": "https://insurance.utah.gov/consumers/home-insurance/home-claims-info/"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          7,
          8
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help tailor this advice, could you tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What type of damage\nare you claiming (e.g., wind, hail, snow/ice, or a leak)?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "When did the damage occur?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "What is your estimated deductible?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help clarify exactly how to proceed with your insurance carrier."
      }
    ],
